{"reference":"Proverbs 25","book":"Proverbs","chapter":25,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The opening heading presents these sayings as proverbs of Solomon copied or transcribed by officials of Hezekiah, king of Judah. It also places this chapter within a book that explicitly preserves distinct collections and voices, rather than presenting every proverb as part of one undifferentiated sequence.","verse_refs":["Proverbs 25:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_proverbs_025_36ad2a10d882990a83db75ab"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A difficult image","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Verses 21–22 command practical care for an enemy, then speak of heaping coals of fire on the enemy’s head. The precise force of that image is debated. A later use of these verses in Romans 12 places them within a rejection of private vengeance and a call to blessing, peace, and overcoming evil with good; the saying therefore cannot be read as permission for concealed retaliation.","verse_refs":["Proverbs 25:21-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_proverbs_025_659c3e247d722b22b346726e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}